[{:seon.task/name "writes-commit-before-any-wait"
  :seon.task/plan "Audit C #8, the owner's question \"why commit every 18?\": an agent's run-ahead writes commit when it is idle (agent.clj:61), at its next transact! once the 10 ms window passed (:126), and before a merge, a clone or the attempt's end (:206, :436, turn.clj:71). A Seon agent's whole attempt runs inside one eval (seon.turn/work!), so it is never idle: a turn's writes stay uncommitted through the next model call, seconds to minutes, and commit only at the next turn's first write (real3: 19 commits over 335 evals on task-t5-edit-forms). A crash or kill in that window loses exactly the record of what the agent just did, and it breaks the durability law (a write followed by an effect outside the database waits for its flush; a model call is such an effect). Commit before any blocking wait: agent/fx! (every effect), run-tests-forked, await-op!. Then every turn is durable before the agent waits on the outside world. Seen by lane THREADS (2026-10-21): bin/check durable-read fails about 1 run in 3, on main's binary too (2 of 6) and on jolt/threads (1 of 3): the clone :a4 answers its transact! (count 1), root SIGKILLs itself and its process group at once, and after recovery :dur-clone holds no note (seon.check/durable-write, check.clj:712; read at :756). An answered write that crosses a process (the reply to root) must be committed and flushed first; this task's proof should include durable-read 10 runs in a row."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.procs-test/a-turn-is-committed-before-its-model-call}}]
